//---------------all possible ways to split [3,2,1] into two lists
append(X,Y,[3,2,1]).
X = [],
Y = [3, 2, 1]
X = [3],
Y = [2, 1]
X = [3, 2],
Y = [1]
X = [3, 2, 1],
Y = []
false
====================================================================
length([3,2,1],L).
L = 3
--------------------------
length(L,2).
L = [_, _]

Here L is unknown, but its length must be 2.

Therefore Prolog creates:

L = [_, _]

The _ means any value.

For example, all of these have length 2:

[a,b]
[3,7]
[x,y]

So Prolog doesn't know the actual values yet.
